# base_lake_basin: the lake-basin base (BUILD_PLAN 4.4, 4.5, S5 brief B1;
# GAME_DESIGN 11.7). Deer Lake, Lunch, Round, Clear, Hoh and the off-menu
# lakes are composed on it: a small subalpine lake held low in a bowl of
# conifers, seen from its near shore, so the water is a flat band, its far
# edge straight under the trees and its near edge the shore we stand on.
# Open sky above; the far layer is left for the place's skyline (the
# composer's rule 1), and the recipe's prop slots ring the far shore with
# small firs and hemlocks, frame the view with a big tree each side and set
# rocks on the near shore (content/art/recipes.json).
#
# Day colors; dusk, blue hour and night are palette remaps. The sun is high
# in the south-west, so the trees' lit sides and the gravel at the outlet
# are on the right. Each shape is drawn the AGI way: its outline in its own
# color, then a fill from a seed inside, so the draw-in shows the lines and
# then the fills flooding in.
@ sky
# A light day sky: a thin slate top, glacier blue for most of it, and the
# horizon's light, a thin band of snow (4) from row 68 down behind the far
# trees, the far ring's spires rising into it: a lake with no skyline
# gets open sky over its trees with only a glow at the horizon (pink at
# dusk, glacier blue at blue hour, slate at night). A place's skyline
# brings its own deeper band to stand in (skyline_deer_lake_ridge.pic, as
# the Olympus skyline does). Each seam is two rows of checker, no more: a
# dither is texture in a small dose, never a band of screen across the
# sky. Each band is cut by an ink row that the band's fill then takes too
# (as on the cover). The sky stops at row 101, under the far shore, so the
# draw-in never floods it over the water. The stars stay above row 36.
C 0  L 0,5 159,5  L 0,7 159,7  L 0,66 159,66  L 0,68 159,68
     L 0,101 159,101
C 2              F 80,2
D 2 3 checker    F 80,6 80,5
C 3              F 80,20 80,7
D 3 4 checker    F 80,67 80,66
C 4              F 80,80 80,68 80,101
Z at_sky 80,20,1,1
Z at_star_floor 0,36,1,1
@ mid
# The far shore's treeline: a serrated line of fir spires in spruce (11),
# filled spruce down under the water, then each spire's sunlit right edge
# in forest (12). The spires rise steeply, a column or two for six or
# eight rows, so they read as subalpine firs on the wide pixel.
C 11  L 0,80 1,73 2,79 4,74 6,80 8,75 11,79 14,75
        16,81 17,73 20,80 22,76 25,82 27,72 29,79 30,76
        32,81 34,75 36,79 39,75 40,81 41,72 43,82 45,76
        48,79 50,72 53,79 54,74 55,79 57,74 59,79 61,76
        63,79 66,75 68,79 70,74 72,80 74,74 76,82 78,73
        80,79 81,75 84,81 85,76 87,79 89,75 91,81 93,75
        96,81 99,76 102,82 104,76 105,79 107,72 109,80 111,75
        114,82 117,76 118,79 121,74 123,82 125,75 128,81 130,72
        132,80 134,72 136,81 137,76 138,80 140,75 143,79 145,72
        147,79 148,74 150,81 153,76 155,79 156,76 159,82
        159,126 0,126 0,80
C 11  F 80,86
C 12  L 1,73 2,79  L 4,74 6,80  L 8,75 11,79  L 14,75 16,81  L 17,73 20,80
      L 22,76 25,82  L 27,72 29,79  L 30,76 32,81  L 34,75 36,79  L 39,75 40,81
      L 41,72 43,82  L 45,76 48,79  L 50,72 53,79  L 54,74 55,79  L 57,74 59,79
      L 61,76 63,79  L 66,75 68,79  L 70,74 72,80  L 74,74 76,82  L 78,73 80,79
      L 81,75 84,81  L 85,76 87,79  L 89,75 91,81  L 93,75 96,81  L 99,76 102,82
      L 104,76 105,79  L 107,72 109,80  L 111,75 114,82  L 117,76 118,79
      L 121,74 123,82  L 125,75 128,81  L 130,72 132,80  L 134,72 136,81
      L 137,76 138,80  L 140,75 143,79  L 145,72 147,79  L 148,74 150,81
      L 153,76 155,79  L 156,76 159,82
# The lake: glacier blue (3), its far edge straight under the trees, its
# ends coming toward us, its near edge under the shore (the near layer).
C 3  L 24,88 146,88 153,92 159,96 159,126 0,126 0,101 6,97 14,92 24,88
C 3  F 80,110
# The firs' reflection in still water: the treeline upside down in forest
# (12), hanging from the waterline itself (the reflection's top edge is
# the waterline, with no pale line ruled along it), its spires shorter
# than the trees, a few longer where the tallest firs stand; then broken
# by the faint ripples a still lake has, short glacier-blue dashes at a
# few rows, never a ruled stripe across.
C 12  L 24,88 146,88
C 12  L 24,89 26,91 28,94 30,91 33,93 35,91 38,96 40,91 42,93
        44,91 47,98 49,91 51,94 53,91 56,93 58,91 61,95 63,91 66,93
        68,91 71,94 73,91 75,96 77,91 80,93 82,91 85,94 87,91 90,93
        92,91 95,94 97,91 99,93 101,91 104,93 106,91 108,97 110,91
        113,94 115,91 118,93 120,91 123,94 125,91 128,93 130,91 132,97
        134,91 136,96 138,91 141,94 143,91 144,91 146,89
C 12  F 80,90
C 3   L 27,90 31,90  L 58,92 61,92  L 92,90 100,90  L 133,92 136,92
      L 44,94 47,94  L 84,92 87,92  L 115,92 118,92  L 70,90 73,90
# Glints: the lake's shimmer (16), short dashes that never touch, fewer
# and shorter toward the far shore.
C 16  L 40,101 42,101  L 92,100 95,100  L 64,103 67,103  L 122,102 125,102
      L 20,105 23,105  L 80,106 85,106  L 108,107 111,107  L 46,109 51,109
      L 140,108 143,108  L 70,112 74,112  L 98,113 103,113
Z at_far_bank 96,92,1,1
@ near
# The near shore: moss (13), a natural shoreline that wanders, its steps
# uneven: rising a little to the left, a shallow cove in the middle, then
# a gravel point where the trail comes nearest the water, and on to the
# outlet at the right.
C 13  L 0,106 5,106 9,108 15,109 19,108 24,108 28,110 31,112 36,114
        42,115 48,116 53,116 57,118 62,120 68,122 76,123 84,123 90,122 94,120
        97,117 100,115 104,114 107,115 110,117 113,118 118,117 124,117
        129,116 134,114 140,113 147,113 152,112 159,112 159,167 0,167
        0,106
C 13  F 40,150
# The water's edge: a lip of pale gravel (sage), broken where the moss
# comes down to the water, around the cove's head.
C 14  L 0,106 5,106 9,108 15,109 19,108 24,108  L 31,112 36,114 42,115
      L 76,123 84,123 90,122
# The gravel point, sage (14) with a paper-cream (5) tip, row by row.
C 14  L 102,114 105,114  L 100,115 107,115  L 99,116 109,116  L 98,117 110,117
      L 97,118 112,118  L 96,119 111,119  L 97,120 108,120  L 100,121 104,121
C 5   L 103,114 104,114  L 102,115 103,115
# The gravel bank at the outlet, sage, its edge ragged.
C 14  L 128,115 133,115  L 124,116 138,116  L 121,117 142,117  L 120,118 144,118
      L 121,119 141,119  L 123,120 136,120  L 127,121 132,121
# Stones on the shore, half in the water: solid slate (2) bodies, their
# sunlit tops lost in the water's glare, so no stone rings a bright
# middle; and one on the bank, its top glacier blue (3) on the gravel.
C 2   L 53,113 54,113  L 52,114 55,114  L 52,115 55,115
      L 116,114 117,114  L 115,115 117,115  L 115,116 117,116
      L 134,116 136,116  L 133,117 137,117
C 3   L 135,115 136,115
# Heather in bloom around the bank: pink (6) bells on the moss.
C 6   L 118,119  L 117,120  L 119,120  L 146,119  L 145,120  L 147,120
      L 124,122  L 126,122  L 125,121
# The trail, paper cream (5), one worn ribbon: up from our feet at the
# lower left, four pixels wide where we stand, then along the shore to the
# right, narrowing with distance to a thread. Its two edges first, then
# the fill between them (a seed in each stretch), then its middle line,
# which closes the pixels where the edges pinch.
C 5  L 34,167 33,160 32,154 33,148 36,143 42,139 52,135 64,132 78,129
       92,127 106,125 118,123 126,122
     L 40,167 38,160 37,154 37,149 39,145 44,141 54,137 66,134 79,131
       93,128 107,126 118,124 126,122
     L 34,167 40,167
C 5  F 36,160 50,137 66,133 85,129 100,127 112,125
C 5  L 37,160 35,150 38,145 43,141 53,136 65,133 78,130 92,128 106,126
       118,124 126,122
# Grass: a few short tufts, sage on the moss.
C 14  L 10,126 12,126  L 22,140 24,140  L 60,150 62,150  L 76,142 78,142
      L 88,158 90,158  L 14,156 16,156  L 92,138 94,138  L 52,160 54,160
      L 136,134 138,134  L 118,150 120,150  L 110,162 112,162  L 128,146 130,146
Z at_trail_spot 36,150,1,1
Z at_campsite 128,140,1,1
Z at_rock 60,132,1,1
# Look hotspots.
Z lake 12,88,136,34
Z far_shore 0,72,160,18
Z shore 0,122,160,46
